  "course_description": "Seminars arranged around current issues and controversies in American culture. May be repeated as topic changes. .",
  "course_notes": "(Same as AAS 285 3, HLTH 285 2, and HIST 296 2) (This course is an interdisciplinary examination of the history of health activism in the United States from the end of the Civil War to the present. Drawing from history, African American studies, public health, sociology, and science and technology studies, we will examine how communities challenged inequitable health systems through organizing, advocacy, litigation, mutual aid, and direct action. Centering Black health activism while placing it in conversation with Indigenous, disability, reproductive justice, HIV/AIDS, and other social movements, the course asks how ordinary people transformed medicine, public health, and health policy from the ground up. Rather than treating health as a purely biomedical issue, we will investigate how struggles over race, power, citizenship, and justice have shaped both access to care and the meaning of health itself.)",
